WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:15.126
Detective Fix had been watching Mr. Fogg and Aouda since their arrival at the quay.

2
00:00:15.127 --> 00:00:22.245
He approached Mr. Fogg innocently and asked, "Were you not, like me sir, a passenger on the Rangoon?"

3
00:00:22.246 --> 00:00:26.707
"I was," replied Mr. Fogg. "But we have not been introduced."

4
00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:31.654
"Pardon me," said Fix. "I thought I would find your servant here."

5
00:00:31.655 --> 00:00:35.322
"Do you know where he is, sir?" asked Aouda anxiously.

6
00:00:35.323 --> 00:00:40.526
"What?" responded Fix, pretending to be surprised. "He is not with you?"

7
00:00:40.527 --> 00:00:44.873
"No," said Aouda. "We have not seen him since yesterday.

8
00:00:44.874 --> 00:00:47.864
Could he have boarded the Carnatic without us?"

9
00:00:47.865 --> 00:00:52.714
"Did you intend on sailing on the Carnatic as well?" asked Fix.

10
00:00:52.715 --> 00:00:56.329
"I am very disappointed, but I have missed it also.

11
00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:59.636
Apparently there won't be another steamer for a week."

12
00:00:59.637 --> 00:01:05.694
"But there are other vessels in the harbor besides the Carnatic," replied Mr. Fogg with confidence.

13
00:01:05.695 --> 00:01:11.439
He held out his arm to Aouda, and they walked off. Fix grew alarmed.

14
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.983
He had not expected Fogg to be so determined.

15
00:01:14.984 --> 00:01:22.364
For three hours Fix watched Mr. Fogg and Aouda wander the docks looking for a ship. He began to hope again.

16
00:01:22.365 --> 00:01:27.857
Then a sailor approached Mr. Fogg. "Is Your Honor looking for a boat?" he asked.

17
00:01:27.858 --> 00:01:31.329
"Do you have a boat ready to sail?" asked Mr. Fogg.

18
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.285
"Yes, your honor," replied the sailor. "The best in the harbor."

19
00:01:35.286 --> 00:01:39.385
"Will you agree to take me to Yokohama?" asked Mr. Fogg.

20
00:01:39.386 --> 00:01:43.936
"Is Your Honor joking?" said the sailor. "It is impossible."

21
00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:50.243
"I have missed the Carnatic, and I must get to Yokohama by November 14," said Fogg.

22
00:01:50.244 --> 00:01:56.835
"I offer you one-hundred pounds per day and an additional two-hundred pounds if I reach Yokohama in time."

23
00:01:56.836 --> 00:01:59.721
"Are you in earnest?" asked the sailor.

24
00:01:59.722 --> 00:02:02.824
"Very much so," said Mr. Fogg.

25
00:02:02.825 --> 00:02:06.880
The sailor walked away a little distance and gazed out to sea.

26
00:02:06.881 --> 00:02:12.541
He was struggling between the temptation of such a large sum and the danger of traveling so far.

27
00:02:12.542 --> 00:02:19.627
Fix watched in suspense. Mr. Fogg turned to Aouda and asked her, "You would not be afraid, would you, madam?"

28
00:02:19.628 --> 00:02:22.278
"Not with you, Mr. Fogg," she answered.

29
00:02:22.279 --> 00:02:26.389
"Well, sailor?" said Mr. Fogg.

30
00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:32.276
"Your Honor, I could not risk myself, my men, or my little boat on such a long voyage at this time of year.

31
00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:36.673
Besides, we would not reach Yokohama in time," answered the sailor.

32
00:02:36.674 --> 00:02:38.797
Fix breathed more freely.

33
00:02:38.798 --> 00:02:43.606
"But," added the sailor, "it might be arranged another way."

34
00:02:43.607 --> 00:02:45.611
Fix held his breath.

35
00:02:45.612 --> 00:02:48.046
"How?" asked Mr. Fogg.

36
00:02:48.047 --> 00:02:51.194
"By going to Shanghai," said the sailor.

37
00:02:51.195 --> 00:02:57.306
"Sir, I must take the American steamer at Yokohama, not at Shanghai," said Mr. Fogg.

38
00:02:57.307 --> 00:03:01.145
"It isn't necessary to go as far as Yokohama," said the sailor.

39
00:03:01.146 --> 00:03:06.467
"The San Francisco steamer does not start from Yokohama. It begins at Shanghai."

40
00:03:06.468 --> 00:03:09.519
"You are sure of that?" asked Mr. Fogg.

41
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.836
"Perfectly!" replied the sailor.

42
00:03:11.837 --> 00:03:15.310
"And when does the boat leave Shanghai?" asked Fogg.

43
00:03:15.311 --> 00:03:20.732
"On November 11, at seven in the evening," said the sailor. "We have four days before us."

44
00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.765
"And when can we leave?" asked Mr. Fogg.

45
00:03:23.766 --> 00:03:26.451
"In an hour," the sailor replied.

46
00:03:26.452 --> 00:03:30.466
"It is a deal. Are you the master of the boat?" asked Fogg.

47
00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:36.269
"Yes," replied the sailor. "My name is John Bunsby, captain of the Tankadere."

48
00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:41.475
The two men sealed their deal and Mr. Fogg gave John Bunsby a sum of money in advance.

49
00:03:41.476 --> 00:03:46.538
"But poor Passepartout!" urged Aouda. "We can't leave him behind."

50
00:03:46.539 --> 00:03:50.287
"I will do all I can to find him," replied Mr. Fogg.

51
00:03:50.288 --> 00:03:56.977
Before heading out in search of Passepartout, Mr. Fogg offered to let Mr. Fix join them as a passenger on the Tankadere.

52
00:03:56.978 --> 00:04:05.893
Mr. Fix could do nothing but accept. In a nervous state, Fix boarded the Tankadere while Mr. Fogg and Aouda went to the local police station.

53
00:04:05.894 --> 00:04:09.550
Phileas Fogg left Passepartout's description with the police.

54
00:04:09.551 --> 00:04:13.912
He also left a sum of money to be spent in search of him.

55
00:04:13.913 --> 00:04:16.347
By now it was three o'clock.

56
00:04:16.348 --> 00:04:25.165
They had stopped at the hotel, picked up their luggage, and arrived at the Tankadere just as the last of the boat's supplies were being stored.

57
00:04:25.166 --> 00:04:28.718
The Tankadere was a little boat with two tall masts.

58
00:04:28.719 --> 00:04:33.935
Its deck was as white as ivory, and all its brass fittings were polished to a shine.

59
00:04:33.936 --> 00:04:39.955
The boat was as graceful as a racing yacht, and it was obvious the captain took pride in it.

60
00:04:39.956 --> 00:04:43.421
Mr. Fogg and Aouda found Fix already on board.

61
00:04:43.422 --> 00:04:47.488
Below deck was a square cabin with cots set in the walls.

62
00:04:47.489 --> 00:04:53.948
In the center was a table with a swinging lamp above. The accommodations were small but neat.

63
00:04:53.949 --> 00:05:00.204
"I am sorry to have nothing better to offer you," said Mr. Fogg to Fix, who bowed without responding.

64
00:05:00.205 --> 00:05:04.824
The detective felt humiliated for relying on the kindness of Mr. Fogg.

65
00:05:04.825 --> 00:05:09.833
"He may be a rascal," thought Fix, "but he is a polite one!"

66
00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:12.738
The sails were raised at ten minutes past three.

67
00:05:12.739 --> 00:05:19.788
Mr. Fogg and Aouda, who were seated on the deck, cast a last glance at the quay in the hopes of seeing Passepartout.

68
00:05:19.789 --> 00:05:26.164
The Frenchman, however, did not appear.

69
00:05:26.165 --> 00:05:31.817
You can imagine my disappointment when I found out that I had missed seeing Shanghai with Mr. Fogg and Aouda.

70
00:05:31.818 --> 00:05:39.530
Although considering the way Mr. Fogg travels, I guess I wouldn't have seen much of it anyway. Oh, how unfortunate!

71
00:05:39.531 --> 00:05:43.139
For many years Shanghai was a small Chinese port.

72
00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.498
Before the nineteenth century, Asian countries had little to do with the West.

73
00:05:47.499 --> 00:05:49.920
They preferred to trade with each other.

74
00:05:49.921 --> 00:05:55.599
But during the first part of this century, Asian countries felt more pressure to begin trading with the West.

75
00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.770
When countries like China and Japan refused, European and American armies sometimes attacked them!

76
00:06:01.771 --> 00:06:05.397
As if attacking a country would make it more willing to do business with you!

77
00:06:05.398 --> 00:06:11.684
In 1839 Britain and China went to war over the opium trade, and three years later, Britain won.

78
00:06:11.685 --> 00:06:16.164
As a result, the port of Shanghai was opened to international trade.

79
00:06:16.165 --> 00:06:24.251
Many foreigners settled there, mainly British, French, and American citizens.
